{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>We have become.</b> Like people over whom You have never ruled." "<b>If only You had torn the heavens.</b> Some say it concerns the giving of the Torah, as if You had not torn the heavens. But the truth is that it is according to its plain meaning, like 'If only My people would listen to Me' (Psalms 81:14). And this is the sense: if only You had torn the heavens and come down — the reference being to the decrees — then the mountains would drip before You, the meaning being the kings who sit secure." "The mem receives a dagesh in <b>נזולו</b> because it is the end of a verse, on the pattern of ולא יכלו (Exodus 8:14)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 63, :notes {1 "Ibn Ezra quotes 'if My people would listen to Me' from Psalms 81:14; the citation in the Hebrew text reads פ\"ה י\"ד (85:14), apparently a typographical error for פ\"א י\"ד."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:00:54.414951199Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}